Use of Arrays in Real Systems

Arrays are used in real systems to store and manage multiple values of the same data type in an organized way. 
They provide fast access to elements using an index, making them useful when large amounts of data need to be processed efficiently.

For example, operating systems use arrays to manage processes, memory blocks, and file information. In games, arrays can store player scores, game objects, maps, or inventory items.
Databases and applications use arrays or similar data structures to temporarily store records and process data. Arrays are also commonly used in image processing, where pixels can be represented as a two-dimensional array.

Because arrays store elements in contiguous memory locations, accessing a particular element is generally very fast. However, their fixed size in languages such as C and C++ can make resizing less convenient.
